steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1782311098
      nanos: 900431333
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "fbfa5003-ad52-4434-82dd-f2e4f2a7edbd"
      step_index: 1
      cascade_id: "fbfa5003-ad52-4434-82dd-f2e4f2a7edbd"
    }
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# SYSTEM INSTRUCTION: System Architecture & Product Blueprint for \"GigBuddy\"\n\nYou are an expert full-stack developer and UI/UX designer specializing in the MERN stack (MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js) and Tailwind CSS. Your task is to architect and generate clean, modular frontend code for \"GigBuddy\"\342\200\224a premium marketplace connecting event planners and gigging musicians.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Product Core Concept & Value Proposition\nGigBuddy is an end-to-end marketplace modeled after the operational mechanics of \'italki\', but built exclusively for the live music ecosystem.\n* **The Market Gap:** Musicians are highly adept at finding grassroots work through their personal circles, but external clients and production teams struggle to find, vet, reliable communication lines, and safely book right-fit artists.\n* **The Solution:** GigBuddy streamlines the discovery pipeline. Event planners can frictionlessly publish detailed \"Open Calls\" or browse verified artist portfolios. The platform removes administrative friction by automating contract generation (Memorandums of Agreement) and structuring secure, refundable holding/payment states.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design System: \"Midnight Studio\" (Dark Mode)\nThe frontend UI must look like a high-end, professional SaaS dashboard built for the entertainment industry (reminiscent of Spotify, Linear, or Splice). Do NOT use default unstyled components or bright, basic colors.\n\nApply these Tailwind classes globally:\n* **App Canvas Background:** `bg-zinc-950`\n* **Card / Container Backgrounds:** `bg-zinc-900`\n* **Dividers & Structural Borders:** `border-zinc-800` (Strictly use crisp borders instead of large, muddy drop-shadows)\n* **Primary High-Contrast Text:** `text-zinc-50`\n* **Secondary Metadata / Subtitles:** `text-zinc-400`\n* **Brand Call-to-Action (CTA):** Electric Violet (`bg-violet-600 hover:bg-violet-500 text-zinc-50 transition-colors`)\n* **Success / Financial Highlights:** Emerald Green (`text-emerald-400` or low-opacity container `bg-emerald-500/10`)\n* **Alert / Strict Constraints:** Soft Amber (`text-amber-400` or container `bg-amber-500/10`)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dual-View Target Component Architecture\nThe MVP revolves around two distinct core user personas. Implement a global sticky toggle state (`role === \'organizer\' || role === \'musician\'`) so the frontend layout shifts cleanly between these two views during development testing.\n\n### View A: Event Planners & Organizers (The Buyers)\n1. **Organizer Dashboard:** \n   - High-level metric trackers (Active Open Calls, Pending Applications, Confirmed Bookings).\n   - A list card container of currently managed events with live status badges.\n2. **The Gig Creator Form:** \n   - An elegant, single-page form to generate open calls. \n   - Inputs: Event Title, Venue Name (e.g., bar environments), Date, Strict Timeline Windows (Soundcheck vs. Set Time Restrictions), Numerical Talent Fee/Budget, and Technical Requirements (Genre tags, Instrument needs, Backline gear provisions).\n\n### View B: Musicians & Sessionists (The Sellers)\n1. **Musician Dashboard:** \n   - Aggregated pipeline summary showing upcoming confirmed dates, pending applications, and earnings projections.\n   - Quick actionable updates to handle multi-band availability data.\n2. **The Gig Marketplace Feed:** \n   - A modern split-pane view. \n   - Left side features a scrollable column of sleek cards representing open gigs with genre tags and budget badges. \n   - Right side dynamically displays the deep logistical profile of the selected gig (exact time restrictions, venue notes, backline status) with a prominent \"Apply with Profile\" CTA button.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Immediate Coding Objective\nBegin by scaffolding the primary React dashboard layouts, client-side routing, and functional view states. Use clean functional components, semantic HTML layouts, and embedded mock-data items matching the system parameters to populate the fields beautifully.\n"
    items {
      text: "# SYSTEM INSTRUCTION: System Architecture & Product Blueprint for \"GigBuddy\"\n\nYou are an expert full-stack developer and UI/UX designer specializing in the MERN stack (MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js) and Tailwind CSS. Your task is to architect and generate clean, modular frontend code for \"GigBuddy\"\342\200\224a premium marketplace connecting event planners and gigging musicians.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Product Core Concept & Value Proposition\nGigBuddy is an end-to-end marketplace modeled after the operational mechanics of \'italki\', but built exclusively for the live music ecosystem.\n* **The Market Gap:** Musicians are highly adept at finding grassroots work through their personal circles, but external clients and production teams struggle to find, vet, reliable communication lines, and safely book right-fit artists.\n* **The Solution:** GigBuddy streamlines the discovery pipeline. Event planners can frictionlessly publish detailed \"Open Calls\" or browse verified artist portfolios. The platform removes administrative friction by automating contract generation (Memorandums of Agreement) and structuring secure, refundable holding/payment states.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design System: \"Midnight Studio\" (Dark Mode)\nThe frontend UI must look like a high-end, professional SaaS dashboard built for the entertainment industry (reminiscent of Spotify, Linear, or Splice). Do NOT use default unstyled components or bright, basic colors.\n\nApply these Tailwind classes globally:\n* **App Canvas Background:** `bg-zinc-950`\n* **Card / Container Backgrounds:** `bg-zinc-900`\n* **Dividers & Structural Borders:** `border-zinc-800` (Strictly use crisp borders instead of large, muddy drop-shadows)\n* **Primary High-Contrast Text:** `text-zinc-50`\n* **Secondary Metadata / Subtitles:** `text-zinc-400`\n* **Brand Call-to-Action (CTA):** Electric Violet (`bg-violet-600 hover:bg-violet-500 text-zinc-50 transition-colors`)\n* **Success / Financial Highlights:** Emerald Green (`text-emerald-400` or low-opacity container `bg-emerald-500/10`)\n* **Alert / Strict Constraints:** Soft Amber (`text-amber-400` or container `bg-amber-500/10`)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dual-View Target Component Architecture\nThe MVP revolves around two distinct core user personas. Implement a global sticky toggle state (`role === \'organizer\' || role === \'musician\'`) so the frontend layout shifts cleanly between these two views during development testing.\n\n### View A: Event Planners & Organizers (The Buyers)\n1. **Organizer Dashboard:** \n   - High-level metric trackers (Active Open Calls, Pending Applications, Confirmed Bookings).\n   - A list card container of currently managed events with live status badges.\n2. **The Gig Creator Form:** \n   - An elegant, single-page form to generate open calls. \n   - Inputs: Event Title, Venue Name (e.g., bar environments), Date, Strict Timeline Windows (Soundcheck vs. Set Time Restrictions), Numerical Talent Fee/Budget, and Technical Requirements (Genre tags, Instrument needs, Backline gear provisions).\n\n### View B: Musicians & Sessionists (The Sellers)\n1. **Musician Dashboard:** \n   - Aggregated pipeline summary showing upcoming confirmed dates, pending applications, and earnings projections.\n   - Quick actionable updates to handle multi-band availability data.\n2. **The Gig Marketplace Feed:** \n   - A modern split-pane view. \n   - Left side features a scrollable column of sleek cards representing open gigs with genre tags and budget badges. \n   - Right side dynamically displays the deep logistical profile of the selected gig (exact time restrictions, venue notes, backline status) with a prominent \"Apply with Profile\" CTA button.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Immediate Coding Objective\nBegin by scaffolding the primary React dashboard layouts, client-side routing, and functional view states. Use clean functional components, semantic HTML layouts, and embedded mock-data items matching the system parameters to populate the fields beautifully.\n"
    }
  }
}
