    thinking: "**Defining GigBuddy\'s Scope**\n\nI\'m focusing on defining the scope for \"GigBuddy\" now. The prompt specifies a MERN stack with Tailwind CSS, so I am concentrating on the front-end architecture first, emphasizing modularity and clean code generation. My primary goal is to establish a strong foundation for a user-friendly and well-structured application, as described by the original prompt.\n\n\n**Establishing Midnight Studio**\n\nI\'ve established the \"Midnight Studio\" design system. The dark mode palette is set: zinc-950 for the background, zinc-900 for cards, and zinc-800 for borders. Primary text uses zinc-50, and metadata is zinc-400. I also decided on violet and emerald for brand and success indicators, respectively, with amber reserved for alerts. Furthermore, I\'ve designed the component architecture, planning a dual-view system with a sticky toggle between organizer and musician roles.\n\n\n**Detailing Market View**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"GigBuddy\" market view for musicians. View B features a split-pane interface with a left column of gig cards and a right column for gig specifics. The left pane provides an overview with genre tags and budget indicators. The right displays detailed logistical data, like time restrictions and backline gear, with an application CTA. I\'m also confirming font imports to `src/index.css`.\n\n\n**Refining Technical Setup**\n\nI\'m now refining the technical setup, realizing this is Tailwind v4 and employing the correct `@theme` extension for typography configurations in `src/index.css`. I\'m also ensuring client-side mock-data storage for a real-time, functional preview experience when a user creates a new gig. Furthermore, I\'m integrating this perfectly with persistent mock data in `localStorage`.\n\n\n**Outlining Feature Sets**\n\nI\'m outlining the feature sets for organizers and musicians, starting with an organizer\'s dashboard for metrics, active gigs, and application management. I\'m focusing on the \"Create Gig\" tab with its form, validation, and rich tag options. I\'m also planning the \"Contracts/Bookings\" tab, which will provide agreement details. The musician\'s side includes a dashboard, schedule, and \"Find Gigs\" tab with application workflows.\n\n\n**Implementing Booking Loop**\n\nNow I\'m connecting the dual-views dynamically so I can finally test the actual booking loop! I\'m also planning the design for the \"My Schedule / MOAs\" tab, incorporating signed gigs, contracts (MoAs), and payment escrow trackers.\n\n**Defining File Structure**\n\nI\'m now implementing a modular file structure as a fantastic strategy to prevent token limitations. First, I\'m creating `/src/types.ts` for shared interfaces such as `Gig`, `Application`, `MusicianProfile`, `MoaContract`, and `AvailabilitySlot`. Second, I\'ll populate `/src/data.ts` with initial mock data for gigs, applications, and musician profiles.\n\n\n**Detailing Core Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing some key components. I\'ve been focusing on high realism in contracts, and I\'ve started on `/src/components/RoleToggle.tsx`, which will be a floating role switcher with smooth animation. I\'m also planning the Organizer and Musician dashboards, along with the GigCreatorForm. I have also designed the GigMarketplace component, a split-pane view with filtering and application workflow, and also designed the MOA Contract modal. Finally, I\'m designing the premium Header.\n\n\n**Designing App Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now designing the application hierarchy, focusing on `/src/App.tsx` as the main state broker. It will combine all state into a unified context or local storage synchronizer, which will route to the active view based on active tab and role. The layout is showing high craftsmanship! I\'m creating `/src/types.ts` now, designing the system types clearly. ID attributes will be included in all meaningful elements for excellent styling. I\'m starting by defining `Gig` and `Application` models with key attributes.\n\n\n"
